purpose: The engine side of the seam. Owns the page; produces display lists, never pixels.

# Notably absent: platform. The engine has no window, no renderer, and no way
# to acquire one. That is what makes moving it into a sandboxed process a
# scheduling decision rather than a rewrite.
# `bindings` and not `js`: the engine owns the interpreter but reaches the
# document through the binding layer like everything else, so there is still
# exactly one path from script to the tree. See docs/adr/0008-dom-bindings.md.
# `csp` alongside `privacy`, and the pairing is the point: privacy answers
# whether a request is allowed by *our* policy and csp whether it is allowed by
# the *page's*. Both are decided here, at one seam, because the alternative is
# each of the four resource paths deciding for itself. See ADR 0020 §3.
allow: util gfx ipc url privacy csp net dom html css layout js bindings storage media

public: DocumentPolicy.h Engine.h EngineDiagnostics.h ImageSelection.h Loader.h ModuleLoader.h Page.h PendingLoad.h SessionHistory.h Subresource.h

extern:

# Raised for Page.h, whose growth is the *interfaces it implements* rather than logic: geometry, media,
# MSE, the style adjuster and now canvas are five declared seams, each one a list of overrides that
# exist so a lower module does not have to see a higher one. The bodies are all in their own
# translation units.
# Raised again for ADR 0038's IndexedDbSource: one more seam, one more Set method, the same shape as
# every one above it.
# Raised for AnimationSource (Element.animate / TD-0021) on the same class that
# already hosts GeometrySource and MediaController — one Page, one clock.
# Raised for click press-target + image recollect (TD-0022/0023): two more
# members and declarations on the Page seam, not a missing module.
max_tu_lines: 980

# Engine is the class most at risk of becoming "the browser". Document,
# navigation history, network, and script each get their own type; Engine stays
# the thing that routes messages to them. This budget is the tripwire.
# Grew by two methods for the timers: how long the loop may sleep, and running
# what is due. Both are queries the loop makes of the page rather than new
# state on Engine, which is why the member count did not move.
# Grew for ADR 0011. The loop now asks the engine four questions it did not
# have to ask when a load blocked: carry it forward, what do I wait on, is
# anything runnable right now, and are you still loading. Those are the loop's
# questions rather than the browser's, which is why they are on the router.
# The ninth member is the navigation in flight -- predicted by name in ADR 0011,
# and a value of its own type so that Engine gained one member and not eight.
# The two extra lines are FollowScriptNavigation, and the comment on it is the
# reason it exists at all rather than a navigation happening where the script
# asked for one: a navigation replaces the document, and doing that with the
# interpreter on the stack is the use-after-free ADR 0026 §3 is written to
# prevent. No new members -- the pending submission lives in the binding layer,
# which is where the script that asked for it is.
# 132 lines for the scroll model, and no new members: routing a wheel is a
# question the page answers, and where the viewport sits is state the page
# already owned. The lines are the two paint entry points -- an ordinary frame,
# and one whose damage is *known* rather than derived, because the display-list
# diff answers "everything changed" for a scroll (every command moved) and
# repainting the window for a wheel notch is the cost ADR 0018 exists to avoid.
# 145 lines for ADR 0017, and no new members. The lines are the key path and
# the comment that is the point of it: dispatch first, default action second.
# Inserting a character is what a keydown does *after* its handlers have run and
# only if none of them cancelled -- which is the whole of what `preventDefault`
# on a keydown means, and what the previous message set could not express.
# The fourteenth method is ConsoleOutput, beside ScriptErrors and forwarded for
# the same reason: from outside, a page that ran and said something and a page
# that threw are the same page without both. It is what lets a test assert that
# a key reached a handler *and* that its default action did not run, which are
# the two halves ADR 0017 §2 separates and the pair that would otherwise only be
# observable as a form submission.
# 155 lines for ScrollByKey, and the comment on it is the point: the arrow and
# page keys were the *chrome's*, which meant a page never saw an ArrowDown and
# `preventDefault` on one meant nothing. A scroll is a default action of a
# keydown (ADR 0017 §2), so it belongs after dispatch and here. No new members.
# The fifteenth public method is FocusDescription, and it is a *diagnostic*:
# what has focus, as one line, for the same reason ScriptErrors and
# ConsoleOutput are forwarded here. Every check from ADR 0017 on is phrased as
# an interaction, and a click that focused the wrong thing renders identically
# to one that worked -- so "where did that click send focus" had no answer
# outside a debugger. 165 lines for it and ScrollByKey.
# 180 lines for ADR 0016's dynamic state, and no new members. The two private
# methods are one decision split in half: what the pointer's position now costs
# the cascade, and doing it. They are separate because a click that ends in a
# layout has already done everything the restyle would have asked for, and
# painting twice for one click is a frame the user sees flicker.
# 205 lines and a tenth member for ADR 0018 §5. The member is the images the
# document asked for *after* the navigation that carried it finished -- an
# `<img loading="lazy">` the user scrolled towards. They cannot live in
# `load_`, which is cleared the moment a navigation ends and exists precisely so
# that a response for a document that is gone is undeliverable. This is the
# first resource this browser fetches outside a navigation and it is the seam
# `fetch` and `XMLHttpRequest` arrive on in session 13, so the two rules it
# establishes -- a navigation clears it, and a request in it keeps the loop
# turning -- are worth the budget. The three new methods are private.
# 225 lines and a tenth member for ADR 0020 §1. The member is the set of
# requests this page's own script made -- a `fetch` -- and it is a set rather
# than a map because everything about one lives in the JavaScript heap where
# the collector can see it: the promise, the abort signal, the handlers. All
# the engine needs is to recognise the id when the answer arrives. It follows
# the two rules `late_images_` established and is the second user of them: a
# navigation clears it, and a request in it keeps the loop turning. The three
# new methods are the `bindings::NetworkSource` implementation, which lives in
# EngineFetch.cpp rather than here for the reason the input path lives in
# EngineInput.cpp.
# 240 lines for ADR 0020 §3-4 and no new members. The three private methods are
# the subresource policy -- how one is fetched, and whether the bytes that
# arrived are the ones the document named -- and they live in
# EngineSubresources.cpp for the reason the fetch path lives in EngineFetch.cpp.
# 290 lines and four more members for ADR 0026 §1-3. The members are the session
# history itself, the document id it compares entries by, the traversal a script
# asked for and has not had yet, and whether the load in flight is a traversal.
# The last three are one fact each and none can be derived: two loads of the same
# URL are two documents and two `pushState` entries on one document are not, a
# traversal from script cannot happen with the interpreter on the stack, and a
# traversal's entry is already in the list at its own index so committing it must
# not push. Six private methods, in EngineHistory.cpp.
# 320 lines and a sixteenth member for ADR 0024's web fonts. The member is the
# faces in flight, and it is a *map* rather than a set because registering one
# needs the family, weight and slant the `@font-face` descriptors declared -- the
# file cannot be asked, since what a `font-family` stack names is the descriptor
# and not what the font calls itself.
# +2 members and +25 header lines for ADR 0021's storage: the two stores, and the
# StorageSource the binding layer reads them through. The partition key is derived
# here and nowhere else, which is why the interface is implemented on this side.
# +2 members and +40 header lines for ADR 0020 §5's WebSockets: the table and its next
# id. The table is the *first* thing this engine owns that outlives a request and not a
# document -- it is cleared by a navigation, which is what makes that true.
# The sixteenth public method is `EvaluateScript`, and it is a *diagnostic*
# rather than a capability: it runs a string in the loaded page's own
# interpreter and answers with what it evaluated to. Reachable only from C++ --
# `microbrowser_snapshot -eval` is the one caller -- so nothing a page can see
# is widened, and `js::Interpreter::Run` was already public.
#
# It earns the slot by what its absence costs. Three sessions of the log end at
# a page that renders wrong with no way to ask it anything: did the custom
# elements upgrade, is that response in the tree, what does this shadow root
# contain. Each is one line of JavaScript, and each was previously answered by
# adding an `fprintf` and rebuilding.
# Raised by 30 header lines and one member for ADR 0038's IndexedDbSource: the
# same shape as every other seam above it -- one private implementation per
# interface method, plus `DatabasesFor` and the `storage::PartitionedIndexedDb`
# member itself, keyed by the page's own partition key like EngineStorage.cpp.
budget: Engine header_lines=530 public_methods=21 members=26
# Raised for deferred location navigation (pending_location_ + replacing_document_)
# — ADR 0026 §3 assign/replace/reload without tearing the document under script.
# The network half, kept off Engine on purpose: policy, sockets, cookies and
# cache are four members that would otherwise be four more reasons for Engine
# to become the browser.
# Started, advanced, and asked what it is waiting on: six of these methods are
# the shape ADR 0011 imposed, and the two that used to return a document are
# gone. Members did not move -- the transport pointer and the owned block
# reason were replaced by the request queue and the answers it needs no network
# for.
# 130 lines and a fifteenth method for ADR 0020. The method is `Cancel`, which
# `AbortController` needs and which `CancelAll` -- the right hammer for a page
# going away -- is no help at all for. The lines are the headers a `fetch`
# reads back off a `Response`: a document load wants one header and reads it
# out of `content_type`, and a page's own request enumerates them.
# Raised for MSE (ADR 0028 §3, session 28). The MediaSource table, the SourceBuffer index, the object
# URL registry and the element->source map all live here for the reason the element states do: they
# are per *document*, they die with a navigation, and Page is already at its member budget. Seven
# members is four maps and a counter, and every one of them is cleared by the same Clear().
# ADR 0014 §5. The class the zero-idle-CPU invariant lives in: `NextDelayMs` answering *nothing* is
# what lets a settled page block forever, and a finished transition left in the map would break it.
# Four maps and a keyframe list, all cleared by the same Clear().
# ADR 0029 §2. The backing stores, the graphics state, and the taint -- which is a security boundary and
# is set at the *draw*: a flag computed at read time would have to re-derive what had been drawn.
# ADR 0022 §1's threads, and the ownership statement is in the header rather than here because it is
# what a reader of the code needs: what a worker owns, what it borrows (nothing), and when it is joined.
budget: Workers       header_lines=140 public_methods=14 members=3
budget: Worker        header_lines=40 public_methods=0 members=12
budget: Delivery      header_lines=20 public_methods=0 members=4
budget: PendingWorkerScript header_lines=15 public_methods=0 members=2
budget: CanvasSurfaces header_lines=110 public_methods=14 members=2
budget: State         header_lines=50 public_methods=4 members=10
budget: Surface       header_lines=30 public_methods=0 members=8
budget: Animations    header_lines=145 public_methods=28 members=9
budget: MediaElements header_lines=120 public_methods=25 members=9
budget: Loader header_lines=145 public_methods=17 members=8
# What a load produced. A struct with no invariant, which is why it is one --
# but a budget so that "what does a caller learn about a response" stays a
# decision. Everything on it is already filtered: what a cross-origin response
# may say is decided in `net`, and nothing here can widen it.
budget: Result header_lines=35 public_methods=0 members=12
# One document: DOM, styles, box tree, and the display list they produce. This
# is the unit a second tab duplicates.
# Member 9 is the document resource plan: linked stylesheet slots, image URLs,
# and decoded image pixels. Keeping those in one value matters because external
# stylesheets are fetched by the loader but cascade in document order with
# inline <style> elements.
# Member 10 is the original form-control state, keyed by element, so reset controls can
# restore DOM-mutated values without smuggling internal attributes into the DOM.
# Member 11 *was* the currently focused text-control element, and it is gone:
# focus is a document property and there is one copy of it, on dom::Document.
# See the note above this class's budget line. Page collects the URLs and the Loader fetches them,
# because resolving a relative href against a base URL is the loader's job and
# parsing is this one's.
# Grew by one for the page's script. One member and not two: an interpreter
# and a binding layer would have taken Page's fan-out to six modules, which the
# lint refused -- correctly, since Page is the class most at risk of becoming
# "the browser". PageScript owns that pair; Page coordinates it.
# One navigation, from asked-for to on-screen. A struct with predicates rather
# than a class: there is no invariant to protect, and the engine is what acts on
# it. See ADR 0011.
# Sixteen fields, and the two newest are the point of the ADR 0011 script
# decision: an `async` script is counted apart from the rest and the first
# frame is a state the load passes through rather than the state it ends in.
# A nineteenth member for the module graph: a module script's *source* arriving
# is not the same event as its imports having arrived, and only the second lets
# evaluation start. Counted apart from `scripts_outstanding` for exactly that
# reason.
# A twentieth for the wall clock. Every other time on this struct is steady --
# deliberately, because a clock correction must not move a deadline -- and
# `performance.timing` is the one interface whose fields are Unix timestamps by
# definition. Recording both at the start of the navigation is what keeps the two
# epochs from being derived from each other, which cannot be done correctly.
budget: PendingLoad header_lines=115 public_methods=8 members=20

# 185 lines and 38 methods for the form lifecycle: taking the submission a
# script asked for, firing `load`, and the private SubmitForm every route to a
# submission now goes through -- a click, the Enter key and a script -- so that
# `preventDefault` on a `submit` event means the same thing to all three. No
# new members: a pending submission is the binding layer's until it is taken.
# Member 13 is the viewport an image is selected for -- the CSS-pixel size and
# the device pixel ratio, as a css::MediaContext. It is a member rather than a
# parameter because two things ask the question at different times: collection,
# which decides what to fetch, and layout, which decides what to draw. Threading
# it through both is how the two end up answering with different viewports and
# an <img> draws nothing, because the URL layout asked for was never fetched.
# The fourteenth member is the layout state, and it is one member rather than
# three: what the last layout ran at, the document version it described, and
# where the viewport sits over it are three facts about one layout. The version
# is the layout-clean flag of ADR 0015 -- a geometry query compares against it
# and runs layout before answering rather than returning last frame's rectangle.
# The scroll offset moved here from Engine for the reason it is on this list at
# all: painting subtracts it and a script's getBoundingClientRect subtracts it,
# and two copies of one offset is the pair that drifts.
# The fifteenth member is the scroll state, and it is one member rather than
# two for the reason the fourteenth is one rather than three: where each
# scrolling element sits and who owes a `scroll` event at the next frame are two
# facts about the same thing. The offsets are keyed by element because the box
# tree is rebuilt on every layout and the offsets must survive it -- ADR 0018 §1
# calls that "state layout consults and does not own". The pending list is empty
# on a settled page, which is what keeps the loop asleep.
# The lines are the scroll seam: routing a wheel with the ancestor chaining of
# §4, the rectangles that do not move with a scroll and therefore break a blit,
# the two GeometrySource commands (`scrollTop =` and `scrollIntoView`), and the
# frame-throttled event. The implementations are in PageScrolling.cpp rather
# than Page.cpp, which is what the translation-unit cap asked for and is the
# right answer: everything else in that file turns a document into boxes, and
# this decides which part of the result is on screen.
# 340 lines and two more methods for the focus model, and one *fewer* member:
# `focused_text_control_` is gone. It was a second copy of focus that only ever
# held a text field, so `input.focus()` from a script and the element a click
# focused were two different facts and a page that focused a field and expected
# to be typed into was broken by construction. The one copy is on
# `dom::Document` (ADR 0017 §4). The methods are the click that focuses and the
# Tab that moves it; the lines are those plus the comment above.
# 400 lines and five more public methods for ADR 0016 §2-3, and no new members
# -- which is the point of the design rather than a coincidence. The hover chain
# is not remembered: it is recomputed from the box tree and written onto the
# elements, so there is no `Element*` here to dangle when a script removes the
# thing the pointer is over. The methods are the two questions (does any rule
# depend on this state, what would recomputing cost), the two writers (the
# pointer's chain, and dropping it), and the paint-only restyle.
# 440 lines and three more public methods for ADR 0018 §5, and no new members:
# the two new maps are fields of the resource plan that was already member nine,
# because "which images does this document want" is one fact and a lazy one that
# is not wanted *yet* is still part of it. The methods are the frame's
# observation step, the images nobody has been handed yet, and the lazy ones
# that have come within reach of the scrollport.
# 450 lines and two more methods for ADR 0020 §1, and no new members: where a
# page's requests go is the engine's to know, and this forwards it to the
# script half like it forwards the geometry source. The methods are that and
# the delivery of one answer.
# 480 lines, 60 methods and a fifteenth member for ADR 0020 §3. The member is
# the document's own Content-Security-Policy, and it is one member rather than
# three because the policy, the origin `'self'` names and the base its relative
# URLs resolve against are three facts about one document -- see
# DocumentPolicy.h. The two methods are `Policy()` and `BaseUrl()`, and the
# second is the interesting one: `<base href>` means this page's base is no
# longer derivable from its address, so the loader has to ask rather than parse
# `Url()` a second time.
# 510 lines and three more methods for ADR 0026 §2, and no new members: a
# same-document navigation is `UpdateUrl` plus two events, and the history it
# belongs to is Engine's. `UpdateUrl` is the one worth naming -- it moves the
# address, recomputes `:target` from the new fragment and moves the base with it,
# which is the whole of what a `pushState` does to a document.
# +2 members and +25 methods for ADR 0028 §1's media element: one state machine per element a
# page has touched, the volume beside it, and the MediaController the binding layer asks
# through. The state machine itself is `media::MediaState` -- pure, and tested on its own.
# Raised for MSE. Twenty-two of these are `bindings::MediaController`'s MSE half, which is a wide
# interface because the API is wide -- each one is a lookup plus a call into src/media, and none of
# them decides anything. Widening the *interface* is the cost of not widening the seam: the
# alternative is bindings naming `media`, which is the boundary ADR 0008 exists for.
# +1 public method for SetIndexedDbSource (ADR 0038), forwarded to PageScript
# like every other Set*Source before it.
# +AsAnimationSource and AnimationSource wiring (TD-0021 / Element.animate).
# +1 member: pointer_down_target_ (UI Events click retarget; youtube Accept).
# +1 public method: ResolveClickActivation.
# +1 public method: RecollectDocumentImages (IO-assigned img.src).
budget: Page   header_lines=990 public_methods=187 members=25
budget: LayoutState header_lines=40 public_methods=0 members=8
# ADR 0031's decoder child and the surface registry it feeds. Two members: the playback coordinator and
# the last composited generation per surface id, which is how a playing video damages the screen when
# the display list is byte-identical.
budget: DecoderClient header_lines=55 public_methods=14 members=6
# Audio sink wiring (TD-0019): ring, clock, pending PCM, UpdateOutput. The sink
# is borrowed from app; members grew with ADR 0028 §4 ownership rather than a
# second video-only path. Dtor clears the sink before the ring dies.
budget: PageVideo      header_lines=120 public_methods=20 members=8
# Text measurement split from Page for fan-out: one coordinator member rather than
# `gfx::TextRenderer` and `layout::FontTextMeasurer` on Page itself.
budget: PageTextContext header_lines=30 public_methods=5 members=2
# The script half of a page: the interpreter, the binding layer, and the
# scripts themselves in document order.
#
# Four of the six members are that ordering problem, and they are the reason
# this is a class rather than a function: a page's scripts must run in the
# order they appear whether each is inline or external, so nothing can run
# until every external one has arrived. A slot per script keeps the order, a
# parallel list says which slots are still owed, and a flag makes Run
# idempotent so a caller that fetches and one that does not can both end with
# it. The stylesheets solve the same problem the same way.
# Seven members: the interpreter, the binding layer, the four that keep the
# scripts in document order, and the timer queue. The timers are here rather
# than on Page because they belong to the script that scheduled them and go
# with it on the next navigation.
# Eight, for the errors a script threw. Run deliberately continues past a
# throw, so without somewhere to put them a page whose every script failed and
# a page with no script at all render identically and report the same thing --
# which is exactly the position youtube.com was diagnosed from, and the reason
# the eighth member is worth more than the budget it costs.
# Grew for the three script timings. Two more methods -- is this one the page
# waits for, and run the ones that landed late -- and the same eight members:
# the slot became a struct rather than the timings becoming three more
# vectors. See ADR 0011.
# The ninth member is the animation frames, and it is deliberately not part of
# the timer queue: a timer is a deadline the page chose and a frame is one the
# browser chose. Both are asked for behind one question -- NextWakeDelay --
# because the loop having to remember to ask twice is how one of them ends up
# not being waited for. See ADR 0011.
# The eighteenth method is Detach, and the lines are its comment. It is worth
# them: a binding layer that outlives its document is a use-after-free on the
# next page's first tree read, and that bug shipped once already.
# Three more methods and the lines are their comments: dispatch a `submit`,
# take the submission a script asked for, and fire `load`. All three are the
# same shape as DispatchClick -- the browser calling into the page, through the
# one layer allowed to. Members did not move.
# The tenth member is the geometry source the binding layer asks its layout
# questions through. Borrowed from the Page that owns this, and null in a
# binding layer with no layout behind it -- which is an absence rather than a
# stub: getBoundingClientRect is then not declared at all. See ADR 0012.
# The twenty-third method is MoveFocus, forwarded to the binding layer for the
# reason DispatchKey is: moving focus fires four events, and the binding layer
# is the only module allowed to run a page's handlers.
# The eleventh member is the network source, borrowed like the geometry source
# and null in a binding layer with no loader behind it -- which is an absence
# rather than a stub: `fetch` is then not declared at all. The twenty-fourth
# method is the delivery of one response, which is false before this page has
# an interpreter, because a response for a document that never ran a script
# has nothing to settle.
# 240 lines, 27 methods and a twelfth member for ADR 0026. The member is the
# HistorySource, borrowed like the other two and handed to the binding layer at
# construction, because `history` is declared or not declared then. The methods
# are the two traversal events plus the setter.
# 265 lines, 31 methods and a thirteenth member for `performance` and
# `PerformanceObserver` (ledger session 50). The member is the Performance object
# and it holds one field -- the epoch -- because the entries, the observers and
# their queued records live in the JavaScript heap where the collector can see
# them. The methods are the clock tick and the two entry sources the engine feeds,
# and those exist because an observer with nothing behind it is the stub ADR 0012
# forbids.
# 320 lines, 35 methods and 17 members for the module graph (ledger session 50,
# ADR 0011's one unanswered design question). Four of the members are one fact
# each and none is derivable: the graph's sources, the dynamic imports handed a
# promise nobody has settled, what has been queued for fetching, and what has
# already been asked for. The methods are the four the engine drives it through
# plus the three private ones in PageModules.cpp. The split is the point -- a
# fetch needs a privacy verdict and a connection pool, and deciding *what* to
# fetch needs to know what a module graph is.
# +1 header line, +1 method and +1 member for SetIndexedDbSource and the
# IndexedDbSource pointer it stores, the same shape as every other Set*Source.
# +SetAnimationSource / animations_ / DeliverFinishedAnimations (TD-0021).
budget: PageScript header_lines=500 public_methods=69 members=32
# This document's policy plus the two things every question about one needs.
# Three members and one of them mutable: a refusal is recorded where it is
# decided, because eight call sites remembering to log is how a browser ends up
# enforcing silently. ADR 0020 §3.
# Grew to 80 lines and a fifth member for ADR 0026: `pushState` moves the
# document's address, and whether that moves the *base* depends on whether a
# `<base href>` claimed it -- an element in the document outranks the address.
# The two methods are UpdateDocumentUrl and IsSameOrigin, and the second is the
# one origin comparison `pushState` makes: it is here rather than in
# `src/bindings` because that module may not see `url`.
budget: DocumentPolicy header_lines=120 public_methods=18 members=5
# Back and forward, for one tab, where the documents are. Three members: the
# entries, the cursor, and the generation counter `history.state`'s memo is keyed
# on. ADR 0026 §1 moved it out of `src/ui` because a `pushState` entry is a URL
# *plus a state object owned by a document*, and the chrome cannot see a document.
budget: SessionHistory header_lines=60 public_methods=18 members=3
budget: HistoryEntry   header_lines=30 public_methods=0 members=5
# The host half of the module loader: what a specifier means, and the table the
# synchronous resolver answers from. Two members -- the document a bare or
# relative specifier resolves against, and the sources. ADR 0011's one unanswered
# design question, answered as a split: a static graph is fetched *before*
# evaluation because the resolver cannot block, and a dynamic `import()` is a
# promise settled on a later turn.
budget: ModuleLoader header_lines=70 public_methods=14 members=2
# What one document needs fetching and what came back. A struct with no invariant,
# budgeted so that growth in "what a document's resources are" stays a decision.
# The shadow sheets are here rather than in the resolver because
# RebuildAuthorStyleSheets throws the resolver away, and a component's styles have
# to come back with it.
# 11 members now: the two web-font ones are the faces the sheets declared and the
# URLs already asked for. Kept here because RebuildAuthorStyleSheets throws the
# parsed sheet away, and a face declared in the first sheet has to survive the
# second arriving.
# 14 members and 62 lines for the parsed-sheet cache. Three of them, and the
# lines are the invalidation rule rather than decoration: a stylesheet's text
# cannot change once it has arrived, so re-tokenizing it on every rebuild was
# waste -- but the cache is keyed by *position* in two lists that are rebuilt
# from the document, so where it is dropped is the whole correctness argument
# and there are exactly three places plus the viewport. A missed one is not a
# slow page, it is the wrong rules, which is why they are named here and at
# each site.
budget: DocumentResources header_lines=65 public_methods=0 members=14
budget: PendingFontFace   header_lines=15 public_methods=0 members=4
